---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/figures-of-speech">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Literature lesson. The foundational lesson How to read a
    story taught you to notice what a text does; this lesson names the machinery and
    asks what it is for. You have met most of these figures — metaphor, irony,
    anaphora — at the foundational tier; here the catalogue gets organised, and the
    analytical question changes from "which figure is this?" to "what did the
    deviation buy?"
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sort the figures into tropes (meaning shifted) and schemes (arrangement shaped);</li>
    <li>analyse a metaphor into tenor and vehicle, and say what the transfer imports;</li>
    <li>apply the part-or-associate test that separates synecdoche from metonymy;</li>
    <li>explain how schemes — anaphora, chiasmus, parallelism — persuade by structure;</li>
    <li>write the earnable essay sentence: name the figure, then state its job.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Figures of speech are <strong>systematic deviations from plain language that do work
    plain language cannot</strong>: they compress comparisons, structure persuasion, and
    perform emotion in sound. They are as old as rhetoric — Aristotle catalogued them, and
    the anonymous <em>Rhetorica ad Herennium</em> (around 90 BCE) listed dozens — and as
    current as every headline, slogan and song lyric you will meet today. You already know
    most of them from the foundational tier; this lesson adds the layer examiners and
    politicians care about: <em>what each figure is for</em>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="scrub twenty centuries of figures at work" widget="timeline" config={fosCatalogueTimeline} />
  <p>
    The organising distinction is between <strong>tropes</strong> and <strong>schemes</strong>.
    A trope changes what words mean (metaphor, irony, metonymy); a scheme changes how words
    are arranged (anaphora, chiasmus, parallelism). Tropes work on content, schemes on
    structure — and the great persuasive passages usually combine both, which is why the
    catalogue pays off most when you read a speech or a soliloquy with both lenses at once.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each figure by its mechanism" widget="sort-buckets" config={fosMechanismBuckets} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="tropes shift meaning, schemes arrange sound, metaphors run cognition" widget="equationStep" config={figuresCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> panels 1 and 2 split the catalogue by mechanism —
    tropes move meaning, schemes arrange sound — and panel 3 makes the deeper claim:
    the figures run ordinary thought, not just poetry. As you read the examples, watch
    which ones you could replace with plain statement and which resist; the resistant
    ones are where a figure is doing work no plain sentence can.
  </p>

  <h2>Tropes: meaning shifted</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Metaphor</strong> asserts that one thing is another, importing the second
    thing's properties into the first. The technical pair helps analysis: the
    <strong>tenor</strong> is the subject, the <strong>vehicle</strong> is what it is
    pictured as. In "Hope is the thing with feathers", hope is tenor, bird is vehicle, and
    the poem works by transferring bird-properties (perching, singing wordless songs,
    enduring a gale) onto the feeling. A <strong>simile</strong> does the same work with its
    connective showing ("my love is like a red, red rose") — the difference is not size but
    posture: metaphor asserts identity and lets the reader feel the shock of it; simile
    keeps the comparison visible, which can be gentler or more wry.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each figure with its tenor-and-vehicle analysis" widget="match" config={fosTenorMatch} />
  <p>
    The rest of the trope family is machinery for reference and attitude.
    <strong>Personification</strong> gives human agency to the non-human — Dickinson's Death
    "kindly stopped" turns mortality into a courteous suitor, converting terror into
    courtliness in one word. <strong>Hyperbole</strong> exaggerates to signal intensity or
    comic disproportion. <strong>Zeugma</strong> yokes two unlike objects to one verb —
    "she left in tears and a taxi" — and the comedy comes from the syntax insisting that
    the emotional and the mundane belong to the same event. <strong>Oxymoron</strong> welds
    contradictions ("O brawling love, O loving hate" — Romeo and Juliet's first scene)
    to stage a divided mind in a single phrase.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the trope family shifts meaning by transferring,
    exaggerating or yoking — with the shock of assertion doing the work. The next pair
    shifts reference instead, and the distinction between them is the most commonly
    confused in the whole catalogue.
  </p>

  <h2>Metonymy and synecdoche: the machinery of reference</h2>
  <p>
    These two are the most confused pair in the catalogue, and the distinction is worth
    getting exactly right. <strong>Metonymy</strong> substitutes a closely associated thing
    for the thing meant: "the Crown decided" (the monarch or government), "the pen is
    mightier than the sword" (writing beats force), "the whole town turned out". The
    substitute is an <em>associate</em>, not a part. <strong>Synecdoche</strong> is the
    part-for-whole (or whole-for-part) version: "all hands on deck" counts sailors by their
    working hands; "nice wheels" praises a car by its tyres; "boots on the ground" counts
    soldiers the same way.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="walk the part-or-associate test" widget="branch" config={fosTestBranch} />
  <p>
    The test: is the substituted thing a <em>part or member</em> of the thing meant
    (synecdoche), or merely <em>connected with it</em> (metonymy)? The stakes are not
    pedantic — these figures are how power names things. Calling workers "hands" makes
    labour a commodity you can hire by the dozen; calling a war's dead "collateral damage"
    hides people inside logistics. When a writer hands you a metonym, ask who benefits
    from the substitution. That question turns the figure from trivia into criticism.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> metonymy and metaphor are not the same. In metaphor the
  vehicle comes from a <em>different conceptual domain</em> (time pictured as money); in
  metonymy the substitute comes from the <em>same domain</em> — a real crown, a real pen,
  standing next to the thing it names. Metaphor transfers across domains; metonymy points
  within one.</p>

  <h2>Irony and understatement</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Irony</strong> means one thing while saying another, and it is the trope most
    often mislabelled. Verbal irony depends on a gap the reader is invited to see over the
    speaker's head: Jonathan Swift calmly proposing to solve Irish poverty by eating babies
    ("A Modest Proposal", 1729) is irony carrying a moral argument so fierce that plain
    statement would have failed. <strong>Dramatic irony</strong> is the stage version — the
    audience knows what the speaker does not (Duncan walking gratefully into Macbeth's
    castle: "this castle hath a pleasant seat") — and it makes the reader complicit with the
    author rather than the character.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the line the definition refutes" widget="spot-error" config={fosIronySpot} />
  <p>
    Understatement is irony's quiet cousin. <strong>Litotes</strong> affirms by negating the
    opposite: "not bad" for excellent, "no mean city" for a great one — the gap between
    phrasing and fact creates either dry comedy or steel (a soldier calling a bombardment
    "not ideal"). <strong>Euphemism</strong> works the same gap socially: "passed away",
    "downsizing", "let go". The figure chosen tells you the speaker's project — consolation,
    evasion, or spin — which is why rhetoricians treat euphemism as data about power, not
    politeness.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "irony just means sarcasm — or an unlucky
  coincidence." Verbal irony is a gap the reader is invited to see over the speaker's
  head; sarcasm is only its sharpest spoken form, and dramatic irony needs no speaker
  intending anything at all: the audience simply knows what Duncan does not. The gap,
  not the tone of voice, is the definition.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> tropes work on what words mean, and the understatement
    family shows the gap between phrasing and fact can console, evade or spin. Schemes
    now move the action from word choice to word arrangement.
  </p>

  <h2>Schemes: structure as persuasion</h2>
  <p>
    Schemes are figures of arrangement, and they persuade by making an idea <em>felt</em>
    before it is judged. <strong>Anaphora</strong> — repeated openings — builds rhythm into
    insistence: Churchill in June 1940, "We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on
    the landing grounds, we shall fight in the fields and in the streets", turns a military
    position into a drumbeat of refusal. The repetition is not emphasis alone; each return
    widens the terrain until the sentence has defended the whole island.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Chiasmus</strong> reverses the order of words across two clauses — Kennedy's
    "ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country" — and
    the ABBA structure makes the reversal audible: the listener hears the priorities flip.
    <strong>Parallelism</strong> sets matched clauses in a row so the differences between
    them pop ("It was the best of times, it was the worst of times" — Dickens's antithesis
    runs on parallel rails). <strong>Asyndeton</strong> strips the connectives — "veni,
    vidi, vici" — for speed and finality; <strong>polysyndeton</strong> piles them on —
    "and… and… and…" — for accumulation and fatigue. The scheme is the speed setting of
    prose, and skilled writers change gears deliberately.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is a survival skill: metaphor runs cognition</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest modern finding about figures is Lakoff and Johnson's <em>Metaphors We Live
    By</em> (1980): metaphor is not decoration but the structure of ordinary thought. We
    conceptualise time as money (so we spend, waste and budget it), arguments as war (so we
    win, lose and attack positions), ideas as food (so arguments are juicy, half-baked or
    easy to swallow). The metaphors a culture runs determine what seems reasonable inside
    it — which is why recognising them is not an English-room exercise but a citizenship
    skill.
  </p>
  <p>
    Political language is a war of frames, and the figures are the weapons. "Death tax"
    versus "estate tax" describes one law with two metaphors and produces different poll
    numbers; "tax relief" presupposes that tax is an affliction before any argument begins;
    "pro-life" and "pro-choice" each quietly allocate the virtue. Naming the figure
    disarms it: once you can say "that is a metonymy hiding people inside logistics", the
    phrase loses its anaesthetic. This is the same reflex the statistics ladder builds for
    numbers — applied to words.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — the frame before and after naming" widget="compare" config={fosFrameCompare} />

  <h2>Working the purpose question</h2>
  <p>
    In an essay, the figure never earns marks by being named. The earnable sentence has
    this shape: <em>name the figure, then state its job</em>. Weak: "The poet uses
    personification." Strong: "Personifying Death as a courteous suitor lets the poem
    rehearse acceptance — the terror is converted into etiquette, and the poem's calm is
    the figure's doing." Two sentences, one of them doing all the work. When you revise,
    check every named figure in your draft and ask: could plain statement do this job? If
    yes, the figure was decoration and the analysis is empty. If no, you have found the
    sentence the examiner is waiting for.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="rebuild the essay check, step by step" widget="order" config={fosEssayOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "naming the figure is the analysis." A spotter's
  list earns nothing: the marks come from the second sentence, which states what the
  figure accomplishes that plain statement could not. If a paraphrase without the
  figure loses nothing, the figure was decoration; if it loses something, you have
  found the analysis.</p>

  <h2>Symbols, motifs and extended metaphors</h2>
  <p>
    Three related terms need separating because examiners do. A <strong>metaphor</strong>
    is a local transfer: one clause, one tenor, one vehicle. A <strong>symbol</strong> is an
    image that carries meaning without being explicitly compared — the green light in The
    Great Gatsby, the conch in Lord of the Flies — and it accrues meaning by recurrence
    rather than statement. A <strong>motif</strong> is the recurring element itself; when a
    motif consistently carries the same symbolic charge, you have an
    <strong>extended metaphor</strong>. Macbeth's "sleep that knits up the ravelled sleeve
    of care" is a metaphor; the murdered sleep that stalks the rest of the play is an
    extended one — the figure grows arms and legs across acts. The analytical payoff: with
    a local metaphor you analyse the transfer; with a symbol you track a history, asking
    how each appearance adds to the last. Same discipline as close reading, longer leash.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more device is worth naming because it is everywhere and often missed:
    <strong>allusion</strong> — the borrowed reference. When Eliot's The Waste Land begins
    "April is the cruellest month", it is rewriting Chaucer's "Whan that Aprill with his
    shoures soote", and the poem means both Aprils at once. Allusion is quotation made
    load-bearing: it imports a whole earlier text as context. In an exam, name the source
    text if you know it, then — as always — say what the borrowing buys.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="fill the missing word in the definition" widget="cloze" config={fosAllusionCloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>"The pen is mightier than the sword" — pen and sword stand for writing and force. Which figure is this, and why is it not synecdoche?</li>
    <li>Kennedy's "ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country" reverses its own first clause. Name the figure and say what the reversal does.</li>
    <li>Saying a flat tyre was "not ideal" is which figure, and where does the effect come from?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Metonymy — the substitute is closely associated
    with the thing meant, not a part or member of it; a pen is the instrument of
    writing, so the synecdoche test fails. (2) Chiasmus: the ABBA order mirrors the
    words across the reversal, making the swap between "you" and "your country"
    audible — the structure performs the argument. (3) Litotes — affirmation by
    negating the opposite; the gap between the small phrasing and the real trouble
    produces the dry, comic or steely effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Figures are systematic deviations from plain language that do work plain language cannot.</li>
    <li>Tropes shift meaning (metaphor, metonymy, irony); schemes arrange structure (anaphora, chiasmus, parallelism).</li>
    <li>Metaphor transfers across domains; metonymy points within one — the domain test separates them.</li>
    <li>The part-or-associate test separates synecdoche from metonymy, and the choice is often political.</li>
    <li>Schemes persuade by making an idea felt: repetition widens, reversal flips, parallelism makes differences pop.</li>
    <li>Metaphor runs ordinary thought (Lakoff and Johnson), so naming a frame disarms it.</li>
    <li>The earnable sentence names the figure and states its job — never the name alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Close reading trains exactly the two-sentence habit this page
    formalised, and Poetry: form and meter applies it to arrangement in sound and
    line. Shakespeare's craft shows the whole catalogue working under pressure in one
    playwright's verse.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Dead metaphors</strong> are the interesting case: expressions so worn ("the leg
    of the table", "grasping an idea") that we forget they were ever figures. Where no
    literal word exists at all, linguists call it <strong>catachresis</strong> — language
    had a hole, and a metaphor filled it permanently. This explains why "find the metaphor"
    is a skill with a ceiling: much of a language's metaphor is fossilised into grammar,
    which is exactly Lakoff's point about thought running on invisible figures.
  </p>
  <p>
    At the literary end sits the <strong>conceit</strong> — a metaphor extended until it
    becomes an argument. Donne's "A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning" develops two lovers
    as the legs of a drafting compass: they are two, "but two, so / As stiff twin compasses
    are two", the fixed foot leaning to hear the moving one and "growing erect" as it comes
    home — geometry made to carry fidelity. Empson's <em>Seven Types of Ambiguity</em>
    (1930) supplies the theoretical frame: the best figures hold several meanings at once,
    and mean them all — a pun, the lowest figure in the joke hierarchy and the highest in
    poetry, is two arguments for one word's price, which is why puns resist translation
    completely. Richard III's opening ("Made glorious summer by this sun of York") prints
    "sun" but is heard as "son" too — a coronation and a joke in two syllables. And the catalogue itself keeps growing — every generation coins
    names for its new distortions, from Orwellian doublespeak to advertising's
    weasel-words. Learn the twenty workhorses in this lesson; then learn to ask the only
    question that never goes out of date: <em>what did the deviation buy?</em>
  </p>
